---@class gcp.unstable.servicecontrol_v1.ReportResponse
---@field serviceRolloutId? string The current service rollout id used to process the request.
---@field serviceConfigId? string The actual config id used to process the request.
---@field reportErrors? gcp.unstable.servicecontrol_v1.ReportErrorList Partial failures, one for each `Operation` in the request that failed processing. There are three possible combinations of the RPC status: 1. The combination of a successful RPC status and an empty `report_errors` list indicates a complete success where all `Operations` in the request are processed successfully. 2. The combination of a successful RPC status and a non-empty `report_errors` list indicates a partial success where some `Operations` in the request succeeded. Each `Operation` that failed processing has a corresponding item in this list. 3. A failed RPC status indicates a general non-deterministic failure. When this happens, it's impossible to know which of the 'Operations' in the request succeeded or failed.

---@class gcp.unstable.servicecontrol_v1.ReportServicesRequest
---@field operations? gcp.unstable.servicecontrol_v1.OperationList Operations to be reported. Typically the service should report one operation per request. Putting multiple operations into a single request is allowed, but should be used only when multiple operations are natually available at the time of the report. There is no limit on the number of operations in the same ReportRequest, however the ReportRequest size should be no larger than 1MB. See ReportResponse.report_errors for partial failure behavior.
---@field serviceConfigId? string Specifies which version of service config should be used to process the request. If unspecified or no matching version can be found, the latest one will be used.
---@field serviceName string The service name as specified in its service configuration. For example, `"pubsub.googleapis.com"`. See [google.api.Service](https://cloud.google.com/service-management/reference/rpc/google.api#google.api.Service) for the definition of a service name.

